WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.970
- People are coming in and hugging now.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.390
And I'm like, "Are we allowed to hug?

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.223
Is it over?"

00:00:04.223 --> 00:00:05.410
- I think-
- I'm not sure.

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.800
- Because I'm back and
forth the UK at the minute,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.219
and we're a bit ahead
of you here in the UK

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:11.204
in terms of like-
- You're the future.

00:00:11.204 --> 00:00:12.210
- We're kind of in the future.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.800
- I always feel like a
jerk when people come on

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.446
and I'm like, "Here's my elbow."

00:00:15.446 --> 00:00:16.520
- Oh, I know.
- Yeah.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.850
You feel like a tool, man.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.760
- No, but nobody's tested more than us.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.280
- Absolutely.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.810
Wait, when you were here in November,

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.390
you did something back
in our dressing room

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.810
that I found out about later.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.130
And so we have a tape.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.645
Let's see this.
- Okay.

00:00:28.645 --> 00:00:33.645
♪ Why are there so many
songs about rainbows ♪

00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:39.044
♪ What's on the other side ♪

00:00:39.044 --> 00:00:44.044
♪ Rainbows are visions,
but only illusions ♪

00:00:44.409 --> 00:00:47.875
♪ Rainbows have nothing to hide ♪

00:00:47.875 --> 00:00:50.833
(audience applauds)

00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:52.147
- So there's two things.

00:00:52.147 --> 00:00:54.820
One, it's an amazing
Kermit the Frog impression.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.950
Two, I am the only human, I guess,

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.400
that missed that song in childhood,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.350
'cause somebody did it on "The Voice,"

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:01.237
and the other coaches were like,

00:01:01.237 --> 00:01:02.420
"How do you not know this song?"

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.650
And I was like, "I missed
this song somehow."

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.640
And it's like a huge song for, like-

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.282
- That's a huge song, yeah.
- Yeah, I missed it.

00:01:08.282 --> 00:01:09.402
- Paul Williams wrote it.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:10.956
He wrote, like, all the
songs for the Carpenters.

00:01:10.956 --> 00:01:12.143
- It's beautiful.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:12.143 --> 00:01:14.530
- He wrote all the music
for "Bugsy Malone," amazing.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:15.830
- It sounds like Karen Carpenter.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:16.750
It sounds like that vibe.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:17.690
- It does have that vibe.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.273
But my favorite version of it, there was,

00:01:19.273 --> 00:01:21.310
that I'm obsessed with this YouTube clip,

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:22.300
I watch it all the time,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.550
is of Debbie Harry singing
it on, like, "Sesame Street"

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:26.565
back in the seventies.

00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:27.398
- [Kelly] I've never heard that one.

00:01:27.398 --> 00:01:28.590
- It's incredible.
- Okay.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:29.423
- It's really incredible.

00:01:29.423 --> 00:01:30.350
She's like, it's like her

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:32.230
and her absolute pumped,
like, she looks amazing.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.063
She's singing it so cute.

00:01:33.063 --> 00:01:34.190
- Yeah.
- It's amazing.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.023
- Oh, I love that.

00:01:35.023 --> 00:01:36.407
I know, it was so funny you
saying that, I was like,

00:01:36.407 --> 00:01:39.880
"Am I literally the only human
that missed it as a child?"

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.910
I don't know how I-
- Yeah, you missed it.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.720
- I know, a breath childhood.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.810
So, can you do any other impressions?

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:45.880
Are you, like, a good impressionist?

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.180
- No.
- Me neither.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.220
- I'm terrible at it.
- Oh, my God.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.690
Do you get frightened in interviews?

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.480
They always ask me, they're
like, "Can you do an accent?"

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.790
And I'm like, "No, 'cause
I'm not Meryl Streep."

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:54.623
Like, what?

00:01:54.623 --> 00:01:55.950
Like, I'm just, like, a singer.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.783
Like what?

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:57.960
- But, I'm an actor.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.750
Like, I'm meant to be able
to do it all the time.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:00.870
You know what I think it is?

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:01.703
- But actors can do it.

00:02:01.703 --> 00:02:04.810
- I can mimic, like, I can
do it, like, straight away

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:05.643
after I'm-
- Me, okay.

00:02:05.643 --> 00:02:07.930
If somebody does it, I can do it, yes.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:09.270
- And if I'm doing it for a job,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.590
and I've had all months to
prepare, yeah, sure, of course.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.330
But, I can't just, like, jump
into it like some people can.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:15.530
- Well, it's so funny 'cause to me,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.140
you have just such a thick accent.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.960
So I'm like, that must
be hell to try and lose.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.980
- Yeah.
- I have a thick accent, so-

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.077
- Yeah, it's nice the last
couple things I've done,

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:27.000
"Belfast" and "The Tourist,"
I got to use my own accent.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.170
That's much, much easier.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.260
- It's a cool accent.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.223
You should just use it all the time.

00:02:31.223 --> 00:02:33.544
(audience applauds)

00:02:33.544 --> 00:02:35.120
You have a very musical household,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.130
your wife is a composer.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:37.620
- I remember you were
telling me that before.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.310
So do you sing, do y'all sing,

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:40.550
or is it a very musical household?

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.720
- Yeah, I sing too much,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.337
I think, around the house, you know.

00:02:44.337 --> 00:02:45.780
I'm one of those people-
- She's like, "Shut up."

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.300
- I just, I have a vast energy, I guess.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.400
I'm not very good at being
still and being quiet.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.860
So, I'm always in the
house running around.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:53.950
It's a musical house, right?

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.640
We've got, like, a couple of
pianos in there, a few guitars,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.120
and the kids are now starting
to learn to play instruments.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.937
But, I think I sing a lot and I feel,

00:03:02.937 --> 00:03:04.310
and I don't realize I'm doing it.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.410
So my wife will be,
like, been in the kid's

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.310
and she'll turn around,
like, "Are you serious?"

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:09.261
I'm like, "What?"

00:03:09.261 --> 00:03:11.480
"'Cause you've be singing the same line,

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.668
like, over and over."

00:03:12.668 --> 00:03:13.960
- Oh, you don't know the whole song?

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.250
- Often not, you know?
(Kelly chuckles)

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.220
But, like, none of us, like,

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:18.830
there's probably only about
three songs in the world

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.250
I know all the words to,
do you know what I mean?

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.270
- And they're all from, like,
junior high for some reason,

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.390
like, for some reason we were like 13,

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.100
we could memorize songs and then no.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.640
- Yeah, mine's like Weezer
and stuff like that.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.150
- Yeah, Weezer, yeah.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.010
- Yeah.
- "Say It Ain't So."

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.659
Oh, my God, they're a great
band live, by the way.

00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:34.875
All right, we're back with
Jamie Dornan, y'all give it up!

00:03:34.875 --> 00:03:36.764
(audience applauds)

00:03:36.764 --> 00:03:39.970
All right, that was a look
at his new HBO Max show.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.460
The new show is called "The Tourist."

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.510
So, explain to everybody what it's about.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:46.253
- So, we find this guy, me,

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.500
driving through the Australian Outback,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.170
and he's in this little old car,

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.020
but he's happy, he's singing along to,

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.490
and then he gets involved in
this very intense sort of race,

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.460
sort of hunt with a a big truck.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:08.140
Truck hits him off the
road very deliberately.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:10.680
He wakes up in hospital
after this massive accident.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.310
He has no memory.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.940
He doesn't know who he
is, why he's in Australia,

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.550
why anyone's trying to kill him,

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:19.190
and then we basically spend six hours

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.920
meeting these insane people

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.170
and piecing together who he
is and why he's in Australia.

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:25.820
- I feel like you're in everything good.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:26.653
I love everything.

00:04:26.653 --> 00:04:28.020
And by the way, you look-
- No, I'm not.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.480
- You are.
- Definitely not.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.370
- And you look like a
completely different human

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:32.630
with that haircut.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.080
- That's what's nice about it, you know?

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.170
I shot it straight after
"Belfast" where I'm like,

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.782
I clean, I'm shaven, and all very neat.

00:04:39.782 --> 00:04:40.615
- Who the hell is that, yeah?

00:04:40.615 --> 00:04:43.730
- And then I'm that guy
shaved head, big beard,

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:45.020
and actually, as we kept going,

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.610
my beard just got bigger and bigger,

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:47.620
and more gray.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.026
Like, it's really gray on my beard,

00:04:49.026 --> 00:04:51.400
which is, I'm okay with.
- That's cool.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:52.750
Being okay with it, it's cool.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:53.620
- Yeah, we're both-

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:54.820
- Women don't like a dyed beard.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:55.688
And if they do, they're fake women.

00:04:55.688 --> 00:04:57.420
- No, no, no, no, no.
- Yeah, it's weird.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.253
- Never dye your beard.

00:04:58.253 --> 00:05:01.167
- I know, I always see men
with, I'm like, I just, yeah.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:02.360
"Did you, was it the box?

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:03.193
Did you get it at the box?"

00:05:03.193 --> 00:05:04.973
I'm like, "What did you read?

00:05:04.973 --> 00:05:06.380
Did you brush it on?"

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.100
Like, anyway, it's okay
to be gray, we like it.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.090
- Totally.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.080
- So, do you like shooting in the Outback?

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.810
Because I have several, I've toured there,

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.730
and that can be, like,
an interesting dynamic.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:18.260
Like, everything happening there.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.490
- It's a hostile environment.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:20.323
It's not easy.

00:05:20.323 --> 00:05:21.310
- Yeah, if you're doing outside shows

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:23.690
- We were, like, dust storms, like,

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.053
between all of the shots that
day, particularly that image,

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.370
you know, between shots, we're
all wearing these fly nets,

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:32.100
you know, the flies are rampant,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.090
and I swallowed plenty of flies.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:35.923
Like, really did.

00:05:35.923 --> 00:05:37.380
Like, during takes,
I'm, like, trying to act

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.730
and there's flies, like,
crawling into my mouth inside.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:40.720
- Yes.
- I'm like-

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:42.530
- Perth is the worst.
- "Swallow it and move on."

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:43.435
- Yeah, awful.

00:05:43.435 --> 00:05:44.268
- I don't know where y'all were.

00:05:44.268 --> 00:05:47.170
- We were in, sorry, pretty
sure it was Adelaide.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:48.207
I don't wanna put people
off going to Australia,

00:05:48.207 --> 00:05:50.380
'cause it's a great place.
- I love Australia.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.080
No, I will say too, it's literally,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.910
that's part of my favorite
place to go outside,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.470
I felt like it was one of
the first places I went

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:56.600
and I'm from Texas.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:57.930
I feel like it's a giant Texas,

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.060
like, everyone's so friendly.
- Right, right, right.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:01.793
- But the flies are,
they're out to get you.

00:06:01.793 --> 00:06:04.052
- It's a real thing.
- And they're gigantic.

00:06:04.052 --> 00:06:04.885
- They're huge, aren't they?
- Yeah.

00:06:04.885 --> 00:06:05.870
- They're hungry and they're angry.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.230
- Yeah, I'm like, "Oh, my God."

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:10.040
But that's, yeah, dust
storms, I can't imagine too.

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.920
Like, having to do takes and
your eyes are probably dying.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.550
- It was a lot, you know,
for every department.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:15.840
Everyone's got sand, the dust
getting into cameras and,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.770
you know, it's not an easy, like,

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:20.201
if you're in this nice
controlled studio environment,

00:06:20.201 --> 00:06:21.470
your job's much easier.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:25.220
Like, but it was tough,
but it was rewarding.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.050
- So I heard that your
daughters were impressed

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:29.710
that you got to meet the guy
who made "Encanto." (chuckles)

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:31.760
- Well, listen, so we had the SAG Awards,

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.050
and we'd spent the whole
Sunday morning with the kids,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:36.670
and then had to go pretty early,

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:37.970
start getting ready for the SAG Awards,

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:39.670
and we're like, "Mommy and
Daddy are going out tonight."

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:40.843
And they're like, "No,
no, we don't want you."

00:06:40.843 --> 00:06:43.200
Anyway, the whole meltdown.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.060
We're like, listen,
guess who we might meet?

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:46.893
And they're like, "Who?"

00:06:46.893 --> 00:06:48.300
We're like, "Lin-Manuel Miranda."

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:49.267
And they were like, "No!"

00:06:49.267 --> 00:06:51.200
And I was like, "Yeah, we'll see."

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.810
Anyway, so we go to it, and
Lin-Manuel comes up to me,

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:58.220
and is like, well, he's with,

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:00.330
Andrew Garfield is one of my best friends.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.930
Obviously they worked together
on "Tick, Tick... Boom!"

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:03.840
So Andrew brought him up

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.127
and Lin basically had seen
this movie I did called

00:07:06.127 --> 00:07:07.910
"Barb and Star Go to Vista Del Mar."

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.520
He's seen it over 10 times.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:10.860
So he was the one coming up to me.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.220
I, like, blew my kids' mind.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:15.640
We're obsessed with him in the house.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:16.753
Obsessed with "Encanto."
- Oh, my gosh.

00:07:16.753 --> 00:07:18.198
- Obsessed with "Vivo."

00:07:18.198 --> 00:07:19.031
Like, obsessed.

00:07:19.031 --> 00:07:20.510
- So, I think any-
- "Moana."

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:23.000
- Yes, any person with children,

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.920
you know, Bruno, you know all this,

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:25.753
like, "Tick, Tick... Boom!"

00:07:25.753 --> 00:07:26.910
You know all the songs, yeah.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:27.743
- Constantly.

00:07:27.743 --> 00:07:29.837
It's on at home, watching it-
- And then in your car.

00:07:29.837 --> 00:07:31.550
- And in the car.
- And then in your car.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:32.990
It's the soundtrack.
- Yeah.

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:34.840
But I'm fine with it.
- I am too!

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.852
- Really good with it.
- And I should hate it by now.

00:07:36.852 --> 00:07:38.020
- No, you can't hate it.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.180
- I should seriously have a loathing like,

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.110
Tim McGraw, "Don't Take the
Girl," back in the nineties,

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:43.560
they played that song too much.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:44.530
I should have, like,

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.070
a kind of a hatred for it
at this point, and I don't.

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.810
- But that's a measure of
how good the songs are,

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:49.940
that they don't grate on you,

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:51.190
even if you hear them all the time.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:52.023
- Yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:07:52.023 --> 00:07:54.310
- And it's kinda cool too,
like, to be that talented,

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:55.370
to impress people with Hamilton,

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:56.860
adults, make grown people cry,

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.580
and then touch kids like that.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:07:59.545
That's a rare talent.

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:00.460
- The guy's a proper genius.
(audience applauds)

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:03.070
- But I love that he was,
like, impressed by you.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:04.185
That's a good feeling.

00:08:04.185 --> 00:08:05.018
- I would say it was great, it was mad.

00:08:05.018 --> 00:08:06.880
- You're like, "Wait,
